{
  "definition_id": "2618da2c-5ea1-5d79-8b91-d0b4f0d5b84b",
  "source": "package",
  "name": "Image Intensity Profile (profile)",
  "category": [
    "analysis",
    "star-optics"
  ],
  "description": "Generates an intensity profile plot between 2 points in the image, also known as a cut . The arguments may be provided in any order. The arguments -to=x,y and -from=x,y are mandatory.\n\nThe argument -layer={red | green | blue | lum | col} specifies which channel (or luminance or colour) to plot if the image is color. It may also be used with the -tri option, which generates 3 parallel equispaced profiles each separated by -spacing= pixels, but note that for tri profiles the col option will be treated the same as lum .\n\nThe option -cfa selects CFA mode, which generates 4 profiles: 1 for each CFA channel in a Bayer patterned image. This option cannot be used with color images or mono images with no Bayer pattern, and cannot be used at the same time as the -tri option.\n\nThe option -arcsec causes the x axis to display distance in arcsec, if the necessary metadata is available. If not provided or if metadata is not available, distance will be shown in pixel units.\n\nThe argument -savedat will cause the data files to be saved: the filename will be written to the log. Alternatively the argument -filename= can be used to specify a filename to write the data file to. (The -filename= option implies -savedat .)\n\nThe argument \"-title=My Title\" sets a custom title \"My Title\"\n\nUses fixed input references and writes only invocation-owned outputs. Optional argument values are sent only when their use_* control is true; zero is preserved.",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "name": "image",
      "type": "APF_SCIENCE"
    }
  ],
  "outputs": [
    {
      "name": "analysis",
      "type": "APF_ANALYSIS"
    },
    {
      "name": "files",
      "type": "APF_FILE"
    }
  ],
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "from_pixel",
      "kind": "string",
      "default": "0,0",
      "advanced": false,
      "nullable": false
    },
    {
